# The Saves at the heads of a framed part, where a processor keeps many registers across a call. `clear` sets up its frame late, in the arm that calls. At the head of that arm more values wait for what lies behind the calls than registers are kept: `this`, the eight fields, and the word that the slow road of a private field asks for. That word is read last and by the slow road of the store to `#timer` alone, as a cold operand, so it is the one that waits in a slot: the head of the arm saves it. The testing word leaves that Save out.
# No check is asked for here, as in a build of a user: the slots stored at heads are checked all the same, because a head left out a Save, and the build stops by name. Without that check it would end well and the code would be wrong.
# With four fields every value finds a kept register and nothing waits in a slot. On x86-64 the entry runs out of registers with seven fields and more and sets the frame up itself: its own test is a-save-at-a-head-that-a-cold-operand-alone-reads-and-that-is-left-out-stops-the-build-by-name.ir.
# f4 = clear
# -- source: a-save-at-a-head-that-a-cold-operand-alone-reads-where-more-values-wait-than-registers-are-kept-stops-the-build-by-name.ts
# declare function sink(...values: unknown[]): void;
# declare function cancel(handle: unknown): void;
